<article class="env-theorem" id="49E7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/49E7">Theorem <span data-tag="49E7">6.5.3.12</span>.</a> Let $\calX $ be an $\infty $-topos. The following conditions are equivalent: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
For every $X \in \calX $, every hypercovering $U_{\bullet }$ of $\calX _{/X}$ is effective. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
The $\infty $-topos $\calX $ is hypercomplete. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Suppose that $(1)$ is satisfied. Let $f: U \rightarrow X$ be an $\infty $-connective morphism in $\calX $, and let $f_{\bullet }$ be the constant simplicial object of $\calX _{/X}$ with value $f$. According to Lemma <a href="/tag/477B" data-tag="477B">6.5.3.5</a>, $f$ is a hypercovering of $\calX _{/X}$. Invoking $(1)$, we conclude that $f \simeq | f_{\bullet } |$ is a final object of $\calX _{/X}$; in other words, $f$ is an equivalence. This proves that $(1) \Rightarrow (2)$. 
    <p>
      Conversely, suppose that $\calX $ is hypercomplete. Let $X \in \calX $ be an object and $U_{\bullet }$ a hypercovering of $\calX _{/X}$. Then Lemma <a href="/tag/6EDD" data-tag="6EDD">6.5.3.11</a> implies that $| U_{\bullet } |$ is an $\infty $-connective object of $\calX _{/X}$. Since $\calX $ is hypercomplete, we conclude that $|U_{\bullet }|$ is a final object of $\calX _{/X}$, so that $U_{\bullet }$ is effective. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>